  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Devenny entered Puget Sound passenger transport by purchasing a controlling interest in Puget Sound Excursion Company in 1958. From Seattle, he operated Virginia V with several smaller vessels on charters and excursions, including trips to the former Fort Ward site on Bainbridge Island. He acquired the former federal prison tender J. E. Overlade, converted it for excursion work and renamed it Virginia VI.\n\nHis fleet also included the older vessels El Primero, Aquilo, Imperial and Carolyn M. Maintaining ageing ships to Coast Guard standards was expensive, yet maritime historians credit Devenny with keeping several working far beyond their likely service lives. He sold Virginia VI in 1967 and Virginia V the following year, after a decade in which his commercial excursion operation preserved rare survivors of Puget Sound’s steam era.",
    "html": "<p>Devenny entered Puget Sound passenger transport by purchasing a controlling interest in Puget Sound Excursion Company in 1958. From Seattle, he operated Virginia V with several smaller vessels on charters and excursions, including trips to the former Fort Ward site on Bainbridge Island. He acquired the former federal prison tender J. E. Overlade, converted it for excursion work and renamed it Virginia VI.</p>\r\n<p>His fleet also included the older vessels El Primero, Aquilo, Imperial and Carolyn M. Maintaining ageing ships to Coast Guard standards was expensive, yet maritime historians credit Devenny with keeping several working far beyond their likely service lives. He sold Virginia VI in 1967 and Virginia V the following year, after a decade in which his commercial excursion operation preserved rare survivors of Puget Sound’s steam era.</p>\r\n",
